WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:06.920
In 2019, you’d probably have to stumble
across a group of scientists to hear people

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.490
casually talking about antibodies.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:14.980
Now, discussions of antibodies and immune
response are dinner table conversation.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.590
You might have seen little Y-shaped representations
of antibodies in news articles, but what do

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:26.550
they /really/ look like, and how do they help
us to fight off infection?

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:31.519
In this lesson, I’ll touch on the importance
of antibodies, examine their structure in

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:37.219
detail, and visualize how they interact with
and neutralize a viral protein.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:39.500
Let’s get started!

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.839
Antibodies are proteins produced by B-cells
that are part of our immune systems.

00:00:44.839 --> 00:00:50.940
Antibodies bind to antigens, which is something
the body recognizes as foreign, like a bacteria,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.699
virus, or allergen.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:56.329
The threat doesn’t even need to come from
outside.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:01.889
On a small scale, antibodies can bind to a
tumor cell and facilitate its destruction,

00:01:01.889 --> 00:01:04.790
and this is the basis for some cancer treatments.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:11.570
These Y-shaped molecules bind antigens at
the tips of two identical arms.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.750
The rest of the antibody is made up of constant
domains.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:21.289
The repeated patterns of protein structure
in the constant domains are conserved—that

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:27.329
is, they’re the same—in antibodies of
the same class, and there are five of these

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:31.219
antibody classes, or /isotypes/.

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:37.109
You might be wondering how the body can recognize
a foreign substance that it’s /never/ encountered

00:01:37.109 --> 00:01:38.109
before.

00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:46.670
Well, the amino acid sequence where the antibody
binds the antigen, at the tips here, is variable.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:52.189
While the B-cell is developing, the genes
that code for the antigen binding region,

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:58.439
which I’ve coloring slightly lighter here,
are shuffled up like a deck of cards.

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:05.609
This produces many different amino acid sequences
in this region here, called the variable domain.

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:12.830
Each B-cell therefore produces a unique antibody,
but all antibodies produced by that specific

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.610
B-cell are the same.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:21.470
The first antibodies the B-cell produces are
attached to the cell surface, and if that

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:28.370
B-cell happens to bind an antigen, it can
be stimulated to produce more antibodies,

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.989
some of which can float freely in our bloodstream
and lymphatic systems.

00:02:32.989 --> 00:02:38.100
With that in mind, let’s take a deep dive
into antibody structure.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.230
This is an immunoglobulin G, or IgG antibody
that we’re looking at here.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:50.690
IgG is the main antibody in the blood, and
the only one that can cross the placenta,

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.970
transferring immunity from a mother to a fetus.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:59.450
The antibody is made up of four different
protein chains, two identical /light/ chains,

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.689
shown here in magenta.

00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:08.040
The two larger chains, shown in blue, contain
more amino acid residues and are therefore

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.750
heavier than the light chains.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.750
These /heavy/ chains meet up to form a region
called Fc.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:22.690
Within the Fc, there are two identical carbohydrate
chains, bound to the protein through a covalent

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:27.939
bond with the side chain of an asparagine
amino acid residue.

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:33.390
In addition to stabilizing the immunoglobulin
structure, these carbohydrates may play a

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:38.329
role in antibody secretion and antigen elimination.

00:03:38.329 --> 00:03:43.420
Focusing on the antigen binding region for
a moment, let’s recolor the antibody to

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:49.150
show the domain that binds the antigen in
a softer color scheme, but still show the

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.909
coloring of the heavy and light chains.

00:03:51.909 --> 00:03:58.810
Notice both the heavy and light chain contribute
to the variable domain that binds the antigen.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:04.459
Now, there’s a repeating structural motif
throughout the entire protein.

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:06.670
This is the immunoglobulin fold.

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:13.489
I’ll apply a new color scheme to highlight
each of the 12 immunoglobulin folds in this

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:14.489
structure.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:20.540
Let’s focus in on a single motif to examine
the fold in detail.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.830
The beta sheets twist and tangle, which makes
it challenging to really understand the fold

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.760
when it’s a solid color.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:34.690
I’ll apply a rainbow color scheme, and let’s
reorient this structure so we can follow the

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:38.290
motif from the N-terminus to C-terminus.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:43.860
Conveniently, with the cartoon representation,
we just need to follow the arrows!

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:50.990
The immunoglobulin fold is made up of about
110 amino acid residues that form two tightly

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.750
packed antiparallel beta sheets.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:05:01.180
Starting with the blue N-terminal beta strand,
A, we can follow the secondary structure through

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.630
a short helical segment into strand B, shown
in cyan, a light blue.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:16.290
Now we twist around into strand C, which connects
to the short strand D through a flexible loop.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:23.900
Twisting back around, strand E connects to
strand F through a short 3,10-helix.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.890
We find the seventh strand, strand G, at the
C-terminus.

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:36.530
Finally, there is a disulfide bond covalently
linking two cysteine residues in the B and

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.830
F β-strands.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:46.120
This is the form of the immunoglobulin fold
in every one of the constant domains.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.730
The immunoglobulin fold is slightly different
in the variable domains.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.940
Let’s check out one of those!

00:05:53.940 --> 00:06:00.380
Applying the rainbow color scheme once again,
and focusing on the N-terminal beta strand,

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:02.810
Here’s strand /A/.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:09.210
A flexible loop connects this to strand B,
and again, we’ll need to rotate our structure

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:13.500
to see the connection to strand C, shown in
blue green.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:17.270
Now, there’s quite a difference.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:25.790
This strand connects to two additional strands,
which are named, by convention, C’ and C’’.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:32.440
These continue into strands D and E, which
link around to strands F and G, shown in orange

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.590
and red at the C-terminal end.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:42.290
There are nine strands in the variable domain
immunoglobulin fold, compared to seven in

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.630
the constant domains.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.130
And here’s the disulfide bond linking the
B and F strands.

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:56.180
Antibody structure is cool and all, but seeing
an antibody interact with an antigen is /really/

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.180
interesting.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:02.360
So, let’s take a peek at the antibody–antigen
interaction that’s made the immune system

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:10.060
a topic of casual conversation, and explore
a human antibody that binds to the SARS-CoV-2

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:13.360
coronavirus spike protein.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.350
The spike protein is located on the surface
of the SARS-CoV-2 virus.

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:23.450
It binds to the ACE-2 receptor on human cells
to cause infection.

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:28.620
I’ve got a video on this interaction if
you want to check it out, but this video is

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.020
focused on antibodies.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:36.120
Here, we’re looking at a side view of the
spike protein, and let’s switch from surface

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:42.070
representation to cartoon to view specific
interactions more easily.

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:48.530
Here’s the spike protein with an antibody,
but this isn’t the real structure.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:54.190
I’ve overlapped the whole antibody we were
looking at before with the antibody that binds

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:58.270
to spike to help you orient yourself to this
structure.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:05.910
You see, when studying proteins, structural
biologists rarely look at the entire antibody

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:11.680
because it’s difficult to get a good resolution
structure and see everything clearly.

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:19.770
Instead, many antibodies are cleaved in specific
locations using protease enzymes, and the

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:23.490
structure is solved with an antibody fragment.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:29.970
A specific protease named papain will cleave
the antibody right about here, lopping off

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:34.789
the two antigen-binding fragments, called
Fabs for short.

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:41.979
The Fab consists of the variable domains from
both the heavy and light chain, plus a constant

00:08:41.979 --> 00:08:49.440
domain from each, all conveniently held together
by the disulfide bonds that make up the immunoglobulin

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.629
fold.

00:08:50.629 --> 00:08:56.750
After the protein is cleaved, the Fc domain
can be separated from the Fab domains, and

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.990
the Fabs alone are used in the binding experiment.

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:07.190
Oh and by the way, Fc stands for “crystallizable
fragment”—when researchers had just started

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:12.519
to study the cleavage of antibodies with proteases,
they found that this fragment was easy to

00:09:12.519 --> 00:09:13.519
crystallize.

00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:19.630
Coming back to the structure at hand, let’s
switch over to the actual antibody Fab that’s

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:21.839
bound to spike.

00:09:21.839 --> 00:09:27.730
In this structure, there are actually three
Fabs bound in three different regions of the

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.040
spike protein.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:36.680
This particular antibody binds spike in an
inactive conformation, preventing it from

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:41.089
changing to the shape it needs to adopt to
infect cells.

00:09:41.089 --> 00:09:47.569
But, let’s just focus on one Fab to explore
the molecular interactions that allow this

00:09:47.569 --> 00:09:54.870
antibody to neutralize spike, and recolor
spike white so the red oxygen atoms will be

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.639
easy to see.

00:09:56.639 --> 00:10:02.709
Zooming in on the antibody-spike interface,
we’ll show the amino acids that can interact

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:04.819
as sticks.

00:10:04.819 --> 00:10:10.589
Some of the interactions stabilizing the structure
are hydrogen bonds, like this short, strong

00:10:10.589 --> 00:10:17.610
one that forms between the OH group on tyrosine
and the negatively charged aspartate side

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:18.610
chain.

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:24.360
Remember, in these structures, the hydrogen
atoms aren’t shown.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:30.709
Another side chain/side chain interaction
that promotes antibody binding is this hydrogen

00:10:30.709 --> 00:10:38.930
bond between this negatively charged glutamate
and the hydrogen of the serine hydroxyl group.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:44.800
Side chain-/backbone/ interactions also promote
binding, like the interaction of this positively

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:50.730
charged lysine with this carbonyl group on
the main protein chain.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:56.660
Though weaker, van der Waals interactions
are also important in binding, and we see

00:10:56.660 --> 00:11:04.019
a network of these dispersion forces driving
the interaction of this phenylalanine on spike

00:11:04.019 --> 00:11:10.209
with tyrosine residues on both the heavy and
light chains of the antibody.

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:16.899
Here’s another one, this time between a
phenylalanine from the heavy chain and a tryptophan

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:18.899
on spike.

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:24.790
And another, this time between a tryptophan
and a valine.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:32.440
Antibody-antigen binding kicks off the immune
response that allows the body to fight infection.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:37.579
Part of this is the formation of more B-cells
that produce the antibody.

00:11:37.579 --> 00:11:42.470
As the B-cells divide, some fine-tuning occurs.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:49.450
In a process called somatic hypermutation,
the antigen-binding region undergoes additional

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:56.529
mutations, which change some of the amino
acids, particularly in loop regions called

00:11:56.529 --> 00:11:59.339
CDRs.

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:05.149
Some of these mutated antibodies won’t bind
at all, but some will bind better, and the

00:12:05.149 --> 00:12:12.110
B-cells that produce these superior antibodies
will become long-lived memory B-cells that

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:17.279
can quickly respond to similar antigens in
the future.

00:12:17.279 --> 00:12:23.170
Scientists can also facilitate the process
of superior antibody production.

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:29.430
Understanding molecular interactions can help
us engineer antibodies that can serve as antivirals,

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:32.759
antibiotics, and even cancer treatments.

